---
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import Measured from "../../../components/Measured.astro";
---

<DocsLayout
  docKey="raw"
  title="RAW, DNG and color"
  description="The files Deptheos reads, which RAW decoder to choose, what a stacked DNG keeps, and how color is handled for JPEG, PNG and TIFF stacks."
>
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>RAW, DNG and color</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Deptheos stacks camera RAW and DNG files as linear camera data, without turning them into ordinary
      pictures first, and can save the result as a DNG that you edit like a RAW file. JPEG, PNG and TIFF files
      are stacked as the pictures they are.
    </p>
    <table class="which-table" id="at-a-glance">
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Your frames</th><th scope="col">Save as DNG?</th><th scope="col">How they are stacked</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Camera RAW (CR3, NEF, ARW…), desktop</th><td data-label="Save as DNG?">Yes</td><td>Decoded by LibRaw or Adobe DNG Converter, stacked as linear camera RGB</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">DNG</th><td data-label="Save as DNG?">Yes</td><td>Read with the Adobe DNG SDK, stacked as linear camera RGB</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">JPEG, PNG or TIFF</th><td data-label="Save as DNG?">No</td><td>Stacked as they are, in their own color space; saved as TIFF, PNG or JPEG</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Camera RAW on iPhone and iPad (in beta)</th><td data-label="Save as DNG?">Yes (LibRaw)</td><td>Linear camera RGB with built-in LibRaw (DNG out), or rendered with Apple's Core Image (<a href="#ios">details</a>)</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <section id="workflow">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Developing after stacking</h2>
      <p>
        Converting a bracket to TIFF or JPEG before stacking bakes white balance, tone curves, and clipped
        highlights into every frame. Stacking camera RAW or DNG files directly in Deptheos keeps alignment,
        stacking, and retouching in linear camera RGB, so you can export a 16-bit linear DNG and do your RAW
        editing once, on the finished stack.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="raw-flow" aria-label="Comparison of developing before stacking versus RAW in, DNG out">
      <div class="raw-flow-row">
        <span class="raw-flow-label">Convert first</span>
        <ol class="raw-flow-steps">
          <li>RAW frames</li>
          <li>Develop to TIFF / JPEG</li>
          <li>Stack &amp; retouch</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="raw-flow-note">White balance and highlight recovery are locked in before stacking.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="raw-flow-row raw-flow-dng">
        <span class="raw-flow-label">RAW in, DNG out</span>
        <ol class="raw-flow-steps">
          <li>RAW frames</li>
          <li>Stack &amp; retouch <span>linear RGB</span></li>
          <li>16-bit linear DNG</li>
          <li>Develop in RAW editor</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="raw-flow-note">White balance, exposure, and highlights stay editable on the finished stack.</p>
      </div>
    </figure>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Retouching a RAW stack paints directly from the aligned linear source frames, so painted areas keep the
        same RAW headroom in the exported DNG.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="formats">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>What it reads</h2>
      <p>
        JPEG, PNG, TIFF (8 or 16 bits per channel), DNG, and the camera RAW formats below, which LibRaw reads.
        Whether a particular camera's files are supported is in
        <a href="https://www.libraw.org/supported-cameras">LibRaw's list of cameras</a>. HEIC files open on
        iPhone and iPad only.
      </p>
      <p>
        Transparency isn't used. A frame whose transparent part is only a border, as an aligned export leaves,
        is stacked inside that border; any other transparency is flattened onto white as the frame is read,
        and the stack's notice says so. Results never have an alpha channel.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="choose">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Maker</th><th>Extensions</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th>Canon</th><td>.cr3, .cr2, .crw</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Nikon</th><td>.nef, .nrw</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Sony</th><td>.arw, .srf, .sr2</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Fujifilm</th><td>.raf</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Olympus, OM System</th><td>.orf</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Panasonic, Leica</th><td>.rw2, .raw, .rwl</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Pentax</th><td>.pef</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Samsung</th><td>.srw</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Sigma</th><td>.x3f</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Hasselblad, Phase One, Mamiya, Leaf</th><td>.3fr, .iiq, .mef, .mos</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Kodak, Minolta, Epson</th><td>.dcr, .kdc, .mrw, .erf</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Any (Leica, Pentax, Ricoh, phones, converted files)</th><td>.dng</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        A stack is either all RAW and DNG files or all JPEG, PNG and TIFF; the app refuses a stack that mixes
        the two, because linear frames and finished pictures don't match.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="which">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Which decoder, when</h2>
      <p>
        DNG files are always read with the Adobe DNG SDK, the library Adobe publishes for DNGs, on macOS,
        Windows and Linux, using the color profile stored in the file. For other camera RAW files there are
        two decoders:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>LibRaw</strong>, built into the app and the command line. For color it uses an Adobe camera
          profile for the camera if one is installed: Adobe Standard by default, or Camera Standard, the camera
          maker's look. Deptheos doesn't include any; Lightroom, Camera Raw and Adobe DNG Converter install
          them. Without one, LibRaw's own color matrices are used.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Adobe DNG Converter</strong>, a free program from Adobe for macOS and Windows that
          <a href="https://helpx.adobe.com/camera-raw/using/adobe-dng-converter.html">you install separately</a>.
          It turns each RAW file into a temporary DNG, which the DNG SDK reads, so the frames get Adobe's own
          demosaic and color profile, as Lightroom and Camera Raw give the same files.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The app's default is <strong>Automatic</strong>: the converter when it is installed, LibRaw when it is
        not. The two decoders crop and demosaic differently, so their frames of the same shot don't line up
        pixel for pixel, and a stack uses one throughout. The command line uses LibRaw unless given
        <code>--raw-backend adobe-dng</code>.
      </p>
      <table class="which-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">If you want</th><th scope="col">Use</th><th scope="col">Why</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Frames as Lightroom or Camera Raw would show them</th>
            <td>Adobe DNG Converter</td>
            <td>Adobe's demosaic and color profile, the same as those editors give the original files.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">To start stacking at once, without temporary files</th>
            <td>LibRaw</td>
            <td>
              No conversion step and nothing extra on disk. With an Adobe profile installed, the colors are
              Adobe's, though the demosaic is LibRaw's.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">To stack without installing anything else, or on Linux</th>
            <td>LibRaw</td>
            <td>It is built in; Automatic uses it when the converter is not installed.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <h3 id="settings">The settings</h3>
      <p>
        <em>RAW decoder</em>, under <em>Alignment</em> in the inspector, changes the decoder for one stack; it appears when the stack holds
        camera RAW files and Adobe DNG Converter is installed, since otherwise there is nothing to choose. Settings ▸ RAW holds:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Default RAW decoder</strong>: Automatic, LibRaw or Adobe DNG Converter, for new stacks and for batches.</li>
        <li><strong>Camera profile</strong>: the installed Adobe profile that colors LibRaw's frames: Adobe Standard, Camera Standard, or None (the colors in the file). DNGs and converted frames always use their own. Each render records the one it used.</li>
        <li><strong>Camera profile folders</strong>: more places to look for <code>.dcp</code> profiles, searched before Adobe's folders. A profile is used when its file name starts with the camera's make and model.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Settings ▸ RAW, under Adobe DNG Converter, holds:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Converter</strong>: where the converter is, if it isn't in its default location.</li>
        <li><strong>Parallel conversions</strong>: how many files convert at once.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Uncompressed conversions</strong>: the temporary DNGs are written without compression, so the
          stack runs about a quarter faster, but each takes twice the disk (about 270 MB for a 45 MP frame
          instead of 130 MB). They are kept until the stack has read them for the last time, which for
          Depth is its second pass, so a large stack needs tens of gigabytes free.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Converted files are kept in a cache for a while, so going back to a recent stack, or painting from its
        frames, doesn't convert them again.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="keeps">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>What a DNG result keeps, and what it gives up</h2>
      <p>
        A RAW or DNG stack can be <a href="/docs/export/">exported</a> as a 16-bit linear DNG, with the color
        profile, white balance and camera and lens details of the frame the others were aligned to. Lightroom, Camera Raw and
        other RAW editors open it as a RAW file. It keeps:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Linear values at 16 bits</strong>, so exposure, highlights and shadows can be pulled much further than in a JPEG.</li>
        <li><strong>White balance as a setting.</strong> None was applied to the pixels, so the editor sets it as it would for an original frame.</li>
        <li><strong>The camera's color profile</strong>, so colors match the original frames, and the editor can switch to another profile for the camera.</li>
        <li><strong>The camera and lens details</strong>, which editors use to pick a lens profile for distortion and vignetting. The GPS position is left out.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>It differs from the camera's own RAW files in these ways:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>The demosaic is done.</strong> Aligning frames requires shifting, rotating, and scaling RGB
          pixels, so the sensor's mosaic is demosaiced before stacking. Because the result already has three
          colors per pixel, an editor can't demosaic it again its own way, and features that need the raw
          mosaic, such as Lightroom's Raw Details, aren't offered. Lightroom's Denoise does work on it
          (<a href="https://helpx.adobe.com/lightroom-classic/desktop/process-and-develop-photos/enhance-details.html">Adobe's list</a>).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>One frame's settings stand for the stack.</strong> The profile and white balance are those of the
          frame the others were aligned to, which matters only if the frames weren't shot alike.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Adobe's distortion correction lands slightly off.</strong> Adobe DNG Converter stores the lens's
          distortion correction as an instruction for the editor, and the result carries it over, but the crop to
          the aligned area moves the picture's center, so the correction is applied around a slightly different
          point. Lightroom's own lens profile, chosen from the lens name the result keeps, is unaffected.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Bigger files.</strong> Three values per pixel where a RAW file holds one, so the result is
          larger than one of the camera's files.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Keep the original RAW files for anything that needs the sensor's mosaic. A RAW stack can also be exported
        as 16-bit TIFF or PNG, or as JPEG, rendered to sRGB with the same profile and white balance, which is
        also how the app shows it on screen.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="color">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Color for JPEG, PNG and TIFF</h2>
      <p>
        JPEG, PNG and TIFF frames are stacked on their stored values, so the result is in the color space the
        frames were saved in. An embedded RGB ICC profile, such as Display P3 or Adobe RGB, is carried into the
        exports; an in-camera Adobe RGB JPEG, which names its color space in its EXIF rather than embedding a
        profile, is tagged Adobe RGB. Frames with neither are taken as sRGB. These stacks have no camera color
        profile to save, so they export as TIFF, PNG or JPEG, not DNG.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="ios">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>iPhone and iPad</h2>
      <p>
        The iPhone and iPad app has no Adobe DNG Converter, and offers two RAW decoders, chosen for each stack
        under <em>RAW Decoder</em> in Stack Settings: <strong>Apple</strong>, the default, and
        <strong>LibRaw</strong>. <strong>LibRaw</strong> (built in) keeps camera RAW files linear so the result can be exported as a 16-bit
        linear DNG, while <strong>Apple</strong> decodes them with Core Image to 16-bit sRGB with the camera's
        white balance and Apple's tone curve (exporting as TIFF, PNG, or JPEG). DNG files, including RAW captures
        from the app's own camera, stay linear and can be saved as DNG.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="under-the-hood">
    <div class="prose">
      <details>
        <summary>Under the hood</summary>
        <p>
          Every RAW or DNG frame becomes 16-bit linear camera RGB: demosaiced, scaled from the sensor's black
          level to its white level, turned upright, with no white balance, tone curve or gamma applied. A DNG
          that still holds the sensor's mosaic has its color profile read by the DNG SDK and is demosaiced by
          LibRaw on the desktop, by the SDK itself on iPhone and iPad.
        </p>
        <p>
          Sharpness is always judged on a gamma-encoded copy of each frame, since on linear values bright areas
          would count for far more than dark ones. The <a href="/docs/pyramid/">pyramid</a> also mixes its
          levels of detail on gamma-encoded values and converts the result back to linear;
          <a href="/docs/depth-map/">Depth</a> mixes the linear values directly; the
          <a href="/docs/wavelet/">wavelet</a> method picks detail on a gamma-encoded gray copy and takes each
          pixel's color from one linear frame. All three compute in 32-bit floating point and round to 16 bits
          once, at the end, so a DNG result of any method is linear.
        </p>
        <p>
          Conversion is the cost of Adobe's demosaic. On an
          <Measured path="benchmarks.m5-max-pcb1-dng.label" />, converting the
          <Measured path="benchmarks.m5-max-pcb1-dng.stack.frames" /> CR3 files of a circuit-board stack
          (<Measured path="benchmarks.m5-max-pcb1-dng.stack.megapixels" digits={0} /> MP each) took
          <Measured path="benchmarks.m5-max-pcb1-dng.stack.conversion_seconds" digits={0} /> s and
          <Measured path="benchmarks.m5-max-pcb1-dng.stack.bytes" digits={0} scale={0.000000001} /> GB
          of disk, uncompressed. Stacking them with the pyramid then took
          <Measured path="benchmarks.m5-max-pcb1-dng.runs.pyramid.ecc.ms_per_frame" digits={0} /> ms
          a frame, about as long as the same shots as JPEGs
          (<Measured path="benchmarks.m5-max-pcb1-jpeg.runs.pyramid.ecc.ms_per_frame" digits={0} /> ms),
          so nearly all of the cost is the conversion. LibRaw decodes as it stacks: the same CR3 files took
          <Measured path="benchmarks.m5-max-pcb1-cr3.runs.pyramid.ecc.ms_per_frame" digits={0} /> ms
          a frame, decoding included, with no files written.
        </p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>

<style>
  .raw-flow {
    max-width: 820px;
    margin: var(--s5) auto;
    padding: var(--s5);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    background: var(--panel);
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s5);
  }
  .raw-flow-row {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s2);
  }
  .raw-flow-row + .raw-flow-row {
    padding-top: var(--s5);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
  }
  .raw-flow-label {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--t-xs);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-weight: 500;
  }
  .raw-flow-dng .raw-flow-label {
    color: var(--accent);
  }
  .raw-flow-steps {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--s2);
  }
  .raw-flow-steps li {
    position: relative;
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 6px;
    padding: 6px 11px;
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    border: 1px solid var(--line-strong);
    background: var(--sunken);
    color: var(--fg);
    font-size: var(--t-sm);
    font-weight: 500;
    line-height: 1.3;
  }
  .raw-flow-steps li span {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--t-xs);
    font-weight: 400;
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  .raw-flow-steps li:not(:last-child)::after {
    content: "→";
    position: absolute;
    right: -18px;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-weight: 400;
    pointer-events: none;
  }
  .raw-flow-steps li:not(:last-child) {
    margin-right: 16px;
  }
  .raw-flow-dng .raw-flow-steps li {
    border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 40%, var(--line-strong));
    background: var(--accent-tint);
  }
  .raw-flow-note {
    margin: 2px 0 0;
    font-size: var(--t-sm);
    color: var(--muted);
    line-height: 1.45;
  }
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .raw-flow {
      padding: var(--s4);
    }
    .raw-flow-steps {
      flex-direction: column;
      align-items: stretch;
      gap: var(--s2);
    }
    .raw-flow-steps li:not(:last-child) {
      margin-right: 0;
      margin-bottom: 14px;
    }
    .raw-flow-steps li:not(:last-child)::after {
      content: "↓";
      right: auto;
      left: 14px;
      bottom: -20px;
    }
  }
</style>
